eflect barns, cottonwoods, and wheeling hawks.\\n\\nPeople have always read the river as both road and clock. Its floods mark old walls with brown lines. Its summer shallows reveal sandbars where children build crooked forts. Fishers learn its quiet seams. Boatmen study the changing channels. Farmers listen at night for the pumps that lift water into fields of corn and beans.\\n\\nYet the river keeps a life beyond human plans. Otters slide from muddy banks. Herons wait without motion in the reeds. Willows bend during storms, then rise dripping when the wind passes. After dark, insects hatch in clouds, bats stitch quick paths above the surface, and moonlight breaks into a thousand moving pieces.\\n\\nNear the sea, fresh water meets the tide and slows. Silt settles, grasses thicken, and islands appear where maps once showed open water. The river, no longer rushing, divides into many arms. It carries mountain dust, seeds, branches, and traces of every town it passed. Then it enters the ocean without ceremony. Its name ends at the coast, but its water continues, lifted into clouds, returned as rain, and gathered again in the high country where another quiet current begins.'''\\nprint(len(text.split()))\\nPY\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":100});\ntext(r.output);", "status": "completed"}], "error": null, "incomplete_details": null, "tool_choice": "auto", "parallel_tool_calls": false, "prompt_cache_key": "01a159c1-fa5d-71a0-8aa4-e7750f105fa9", "prompt_cache_retention": "24h", "prompt_cache_options": {"ttl": "30m", "mode": "implicit", "comparison_response_id": "resp_0dfaa81bf29786b1016ad77d74dab48199b7f253b7e80ee7d4"}, "prompt_cache_diagnostics": {"type": "unavailable"}, "text": {"format": {"type": "text"}, "verbosity": "low"}, "top_p": 0.98, "top_logprobs": 0, "temperature": 1.0, "reasoning": {"effort": "low", "context": "all_turns", "summary": null, "mode": "standard"}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 20820, "input_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 11264, "cac