2026-10-20T14:41:15.980146Z TRACE tungstenite::protocol: Received message {"type": "response.output_item.done", "sequence_number": 409, "output_index": 1, "item": {"type": "custom_tool_call", "id": "ctc_0ec10ce250f7effb016ad77d888af4819883ba8030bf33bfcd", "metadata": {"turn_id": "01a159c2-1b1c-75d0-b0bf-baa8fb2d9e91"}, "internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough": {"create_time": 1792507272.5471997, "turn_id": "01a159c2-1b1c-75d0-b0bf-baa8fb2d9e91"}, "call_id": "call_37bcd8ec4301425ab0667ee31130e5aa", "name": "exec", "input": "const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"printf '%s' 'At dawn, the river carries the sky through fields still silver with dew. Its surface seems calm from the road, yet beneath that polished light, currents turn around stones, roots, and fallen branches. Water remembers every obstruction by changing shape, then moves onward without keeping the wound.\\n\\nAlong the bank, reeds lean toward the channel as if listening. A heron waits in the shallows, motionless except for one deliberate step. Minnows flash between shadows. Farther upstream, rain from distant hills arrives quietly, deepening the current before anyone in the village sees a cloud.\\n\\nThe river has served many lives. It watered orchards, carried timber, marked property lines, and offered children a cool refuge in August. Old mills once interrupted its course with wheels and gates. Their stone foundations remain, softened by moss, while the water continues through gaps where machinery stood.\\n\\nIn winter, ice forms first near the edges. The center stays dark and quick, a narrow road that no one can walk. During spring, snowmelt lifts driftwood onto the floodplain and leaves a brown line across tree trunks. By summer, exposed gravel bars become islands for picnics, footprints, and nesting birds.\\n\\nPeople often describe a river as a boundary, but it is also a connection. It joins mountain springs to farms, towns, wetlands, and finally the sea. What enters upstream eventually visits every place below. Mud, seeds, leaves, chemicals, stories, and ashes all travel together.\\n\\nAt dusk, the river releases the reflected sky and turns almost black. Frogs begin calling from the margins. Porch lights appear across the water. The current remains audible after the landscape disappears, steady but never repeated, carrying the day away while bringing another reach of water into its place.' | wc -w\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":1000});\ntext(r.output);", "status": "completed"}}
